  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 119 AM CDT Wed Sep 16 2026\n\nThe latest RAP analysis and satellite/radar imagery show high \npressure centered across the northern Plains early this morning, \nsteadily shifting eastward into the western Great Lakes. Latest \nradar imagery depicts an active thunderstorm belt along a cold \nfront extending from eastern Kansas into southern Lower Michigan, \nwith cirrus streaming northward across central and northeast \nWisconsin. Satellite imagery shows clearing skies across north-\ncentral Wisconsin where patchy ground fog has developed. Further \nwest, shortwave energy moving east across southern Canada will be\nthe primary upper- level driver influencing weather across the \nGreat Lakes over the next 24 to 36 hours.\n\nLight rain tonight into Thursday morning:\nIn the near term, patchy ground fog early this morning over \nnorth-central Wisconsin will lower visibilities to one- quarter to\none-half mile before lifting after 8 AM. High pressure sliding \nquickly across the region today will keep conditions dry, \naccompanied by scattered shallow fair- weather cumulus. Cloud \ncover will increase this evening as weak overrunning moisture \nmoves in ahead of a shortwave moving east across southern Canada. \nRain chances tonight generally range from 40 to 60 percent, but \nprecipitation will be light with 90th percentile amounts only up \nto 0.10 inches. Spotty light showers or drizzle will linger into \nThursday morning before clearing develops Thursday afternoon. \nSeasonable high temperatures ranging from the upper 60s to middle \n70s are expected today and Thursday. \n\nHeavy rainfall potential Friday through Saturday:\nHeavy rainfall late Friday into Saturday represents the most\nsignificant weather hazard during the 7-day forecast period, with\nincreasing potential for localized flooding across central and\neast-central Wisconsin. Shortwave energy advancing across the\nRockies will trigger a 30 to 40 knot low-level jet directed into\ncentral Wisconsin Friday afternoon into Friday night. Strong\ndynamic forcing will overspread the region, characterized by\nupper-level divergence in the right rear quadrant of an upper jet\nstreak, robust mid-level frontogenesis, and strong moisture\nconvergence above the returning warm front. Precipitable water\nvalues are forecast to climb to around 1.70 inches, which sits\nnear the 96th percentile for mid- September. Warm cloud depths in\nexcess of 12,000 feet will favor efficient rainfall processes.\nModel guidance has trended slightly southward over the past 24\nhours, concentrating the heaviest precipitation swath over central\nand east-central Wisconsin. Probability of 1 inch or more of rain\nhas increased to 60 to 75 percent in these areas, while the\nprobability of 2 inches or more has increased to 20 to 45 percent.\nBecause these same locations received heavy rainfall earlier in\nthe week, antecedent soil moisture remains high, elevating the\nthreat of localized urban and small stream flooding. Severe\nweather risk remains low due to meager instability, with\nmost unstable capes generally under 500 J/kg.\n\nAdditional rain chances early next week:\nFollowing the departure of the Friday night and Saturday system, \na brief break in precipitation is expected Saturday night into \nSunday as weak surface ridging passes over the Great Lakes. \nHowever, the progressive mid-level flow will keep the region in an\nunsettled regime heading into early next week. Another upper \nshortwave trough and associated surface system will approach from \nthe Great Plains toward Sunday night and Monday, bringing \nadditional chances for rain showers and scattered thunderstorms \nthrough Tuesday.",
